'''''Elegia tectorum''''', previously ''Chondropetalum tectorum'' or ''Restio tectorum'', more commonly '''Cape thatching reed''', or '''dakriet''' (in [[Afrikaans]]), is a member of the [[restio]] family, [[Restionaceae]]. It is a tufted [[perennial]] growing to between 1.5 and 2.25 m, with [[deciduous]] leaf sheaths.<ref name=Turner>{{cite web|last=Turner|first=Sharon|title=Elegia tectorum|url=http://www.plantzafrica.com/plantefg/elegiatectorum.htm|publisher=South African National Biodiversity|accessdate=19 December 2012|author2=Jamieson, Hanneke|date=August 2001}}</ref> Flowers are less than 3&nbsp;mm long. Petals are smooth or hairy in the upper half. ''E. tectorum'' is found in marshes and seeps on deep sand in the [[Western Cape]] and [[Eastern Cape]] of [[South Africa]].<ref name=Manning>{{cite book|last=Manning|first=John|title=Field Guide to Fynbos|year=2007|publisher=Struik|location=Cape Town|isbn=9781770072657|pages=43–44|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=uG71ye3wQY8C|author2=Paterson-Jones, Colin|accessdate=19 December 2012}}</ref>

The species was originally noted by [[Carl Linnaeus the Younger]]. In 1838, ''E. tectorum'' was noted by Irish botanist [[William Henry Harvey]] (as ''R. Tectorum'') for its use as a thatching material, and in the making of brooms and baskets.<ref name=Harvey>{{cite book|last=Harvey|first=William Henry|title=The Genera of South African Plants: Arranged According to the Natural System|year=1838|publisher=A.S. Robertson|pages=355|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=cS9OAAAAcAAJ}}</ref> Historically, only the taller forms of the species, growing in the surroundings of [[Malmesbury, Western Cape|Malmesbury]], was used for thatching; the more popular thatching reed was ''[[Thamnochortus insignis]]''.<ref name=Turner /> Carl Peter Thunberg also noted the use in thatching in 1793,<ref name=Thunberg>{{cite book|last=Thunberg|first=Carl Peter|title=Travels at the Cape of Good Hope, 1772-1775|year=1986|publisher=The Van Riebeeck Society|location=Cape Town|isbn=9780620109819|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=ocliNOUc20oC|pages=133}}</ref> as did English botanist [[John Lindley]] in 1846.<ref name=Lindley>{{cite book|last=Lindley|first=John|title=The Vegetable Kingdom; Or, the Structure, Classification and Uses of Plants, Illustrated Upon the Natural System|year=1846|publisher=Bradbury and Evans|pages=[https://archive.org/details/vegetablekingdom00lind/page/121 121]|url=https://archive.org/details/vegetablekingdom00lind}}</ref> Thurnberg notes the [[Dutch East India Company]] had a farm at Zeekoe Valley (17&nbsp;km SSE of the [[Castle of Good Hope]]), where the reed was cultivated. He describes its use at the [[Cape of Good Hope]] as follows: {{quote|A bundle or sheath, after it is cut with a sickle is held by the top, and all the shorter stalks that are loose in it, are shaken off from it. The remaining long ones are then spread out in rows to dry, and afterwards tied up in bundles. With this the houses are commonly thatched both in town and in country; and sometimes whole huts are built with it. A roof made of it lasts 20 or 30 years, and would last much longer if the [[Cape Doctor|south-east wind]] did not blow a great deal of dirt between the thatch, in consequence of which it rots the sooner.|Carl Peter Thunberg|Travels at the Cape of Good Hope, 1772-1775}}

''Elegia tectorum'' was later called ''Chondropetalum tectorum'', but [[cladistic]] analysis, conducted by Moline and Linder (2005) found that the genera of ''Chondropetalum'' and ''Dovea'' were imbedded in ''Elegia''.<ref name=Moline />

In cultivation in the UK this plant has gained the [[Royal Horticultural Society]]'s [[Award of Garden Merit]].<ref name = RHSPF>{{cite web | url = https://www.rhs.org.uk/Plants/305893/Elegia-tectorum/Details | title = ''Elegia tectorum'' | website = www.rhs.org | publisher = Royal Horticultural Society | accessdate = 6 June 2020}}</ref>

